{"name":"Finnish Fiord","hex":"#5db0be","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fiord","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of fjord."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fiord"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fjord","blocks":[{"text":"In physical geography, a fjord is a long, relatively narrow sea inlet with steep sides or cliffs in a valley created by a former glacier, which has since become inundated with water. They are the glacial equivalent of drowned river valleys, known as rias. Fjords exist on the coasts of Antarctica, the Arctic, and surrounding landmasses of the northern and southern hemispheres. Areas with extensive fjords demonstrate an extreme example of the coastline paradox: Norway's coastline is estimated to be 29,000 km (18,000 mi) long with its nearly 1,200 fjords, but only 2,500 km (1,600 mi) long when excluding the fjords."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Geirangerfjord_%286-2007%29.jpg/330px-Geirangerfjord_%286-2007%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fjord"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg/500px-Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg/1280px-Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg/500px-Glacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A glacier in eastern Greenland flowing through a fjord carved by the movement of ice","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGlacier_in_eastern_Greenland.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Fjord_genesis.png/500px-Fjord_genesis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Fjord_genesis.png/500px-Fjord_genesis.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration of how a fjord is created","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFjord_genesis.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg/500px-Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg/500px-Odda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fjords often continue as U-shaped valleys above sea level and freshwater fjord lakes: Sørfjorden (Hardanger) can be seen here transitioning into Sandvinvatnet and the Odda Valley. Odda sits on the isthmus, Folgefonna on the right.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOdda_fr%C3%A5_fly.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Muldalsfossen.JPG/500px-Muldalsfossen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Muldalsfossen.JPG/1280px-Muldalsfossen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Muldalsfossen.JPG/500px-Muldalsfossen.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Muldalsfossen waterfall drops several hundred meters from the Muldalen hanging valley to Tafjorden.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuldalsfossen.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png/500px-Weichsel-W%C3%BCrm-Glaciation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedi